    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d236">

                <pb facs="0363" n="275" sameAs="frus:frus1933v05/pg275"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.00/3487</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reed</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1582</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-04-15">April 15, 1933</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        17.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that a bomb
                    exploded close to the residence of Dr. Ferrara, Secretary of State, on the
                    morning of April 13, killing a small boy aged 3 and seriously wounding a man.
                    This bomb was hidden in an abandoned push-cart and was probably intended for Dr.
                    Ferrara’s new and very ostentatious house near the University.</p>
                <p>On the night of April 13 there were numerous bomb explosions, variously estimated
                    at from 11 to 17, in different parts of the city, including 2 churches, a
                    theatre and several schools. A few persons are reported to have been injured but
                    information regarding their number and the extent of their injuries is not
                    available.</p>
                <pb facs="0364" n="276" xml:id="pg276"/>
                <p>According to the local correspondent of the <hi rendition="#italic">New York
                        Times</hi>, who claims to have been an eye witness, the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ley de fuga</hi> was applied by the police yesterday
                    afternoon to two young men, named Valdez Dassou who, he states, were shot down
                    at 29th and “G” Streets in the Vedado.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward L. Reed</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
